    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant",
    "problem": "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-2+5\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$",
    "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932272/assets/fc6a2568ba77ce043ae0d48840ed4707b3e629b8a52c4b5f182c1e23a249fa44.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.190\\text{ }29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)≈\\pi -1.190\\text{ }29≈1.95\n$$\n\nto two decimal places.",
